{
  /*
   * The repository-wide type check: the Node-side sources plus every test.
   *
   * ## Why this config needs the DOM library
   *
   * UPDATED IN 16J-PREUI-E4-C2-B3-B2. It inherited `lib: ["ES2023"]` from the base config, which was right while nothing it covered touched a browser API. The
   * tests now import extension sources directly — eighteen of them do, for the row scanner, the memory cache, the recommendation runtime and the panel modules —
   * and the panel's card renderer is written against `document` and `HTMLElement`.
   *
   * The alternative was to exclude `apps/`, and that is wrong: these tests are type-checked against the very files they exercise, so excluding the extension would
   * silently drop that half of the program from the check rather than move it. Adding the DOM library keeps every file in one program.
   *
   * ## Why the extension's own config is still the stricter one
   *
   * `apps/extension/tsconfig.json` sets `types: []` deliberately, so Node's globals cannot leak into browser code and compile against `node:fs`. That restriction
   * lives there and is unaffected: it is the config that defines what the EXTENSION may reference. This config exists to check the tests, which run under Node and
   * legitimately hold both worlds — a Node test harness driving a browser module.
   */
  "extends": "./tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true,
    "rootDir": ".",
    "lib": ["ES2023", "DOM"],
    "types": ["node"]
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts", "test/**/*.ts"],
  "exclude": ["dist", "packages"]
}
